-          "quote": "**Input:** an ODE field $f:D\\to\\mathbb R^d$ on the open domain $D\\subseteq\\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R^d$, $(t_n,y_n)\\in D$, $h>0$, a finite real state-Jacobian approximation $\\widetilde J_n$, a finite approximation $\\widetilde T_n$ to $\\partial_t f(t_n,y_n)$, stage residual tolerances $\\varepsilon_1,\\varepsilon_2\\ge0$, the selected stored finite set $\\mathbb F$ of [[def-operationwise-real-complex-floating-point-model-with-sqrt-and-fma]], terminating exact field-evaluation and `store` routines, and the terminating certified-norm routine of [[alg-rosenbrock-shifted-qr-solver-with-residual-certificate]]. The exact-storage check returns $v$ if every entry is in $\\mathbb F$ and `unavailable` otherwise; it does not round. Put\n$$\\alpha:=\\frac{1}{2+\\sqrt2}=1-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2},\\qquad W:=I_d-\\alpha h\\widetilde J_n.$$\nThe two-stage method is\n$$W k_1=f(t_n,y_n)+\\alpha h\\widetilde T_n,$$\n$$F_1=f\\!\\left(t_n+\\frac h2,y_n+\\frac h2k_1\\right),\\qquad W q_2=F_1-k_1,\\qquad k_2=q_2+k_1,$$\n$$y_{n+1}=y_n+h k_2.$$\n\nThe residuals $Wk_1-[f(t_n,y_n)+\\alpha h\\widetilde T_n]$ and $Wq_2-(F_1-k_1)$ are update residuals of their linear stage equations in the sense of [[def-implicit-residual-and-algebraic-error-units]]. The coefficient $\\alpha$ and the autonomous two-stage formula are the second-order Rosenbrock formula recorded for ode23s in WIAS Example 2.37. The first-stage $\\alpha h\\widetilde T_n$ term specifies the nonautonomous extension used here; its order is independently established under the stated boundedness hypotheses.\n\n1. Form $W$ by exact outer arithmetic and set $W_s:=\\operatorname{store}(W)$. If storage returns `unavailable`, return $(y_n,\\texttt{representation-unavailable})$. Factor $W_s$ once using [[alg-rosenbrock-shifted-qr-solver-with-residual-certificate]]. If factorization fails, return $(y_n,\\texttt{factorization-failure})$.\n2. Evaluate $f(t_n,y_n)$ and form $b_1=f(t_n,y_n)+\\alpha h\\widetilde T_n$ by exact outer arithmetic. If either evaluation fails or storage $b_{1,s}:=\\operatorname{store}(b_1)$ is unavailable, return $(y_n,\\texttt{input-or-representation-failure})$. Solve $W_sk_1=b_{1,s}$ with tolerance $\\varepsilon_1$. If it is not certified, return $(y_n,\\text{stage-1 status})$. Otherwise set $k_1$ to its returned vector.\n3. Check $(t_n+h/2,y_n+hk_1/2)\\in D$. If not, return $(y_n,\\texttt{invalid-stage-domain})$. Otherwise evaluate $F_1$ exactly, form $b_2=F_1-k_1$ exactly, and set $b_{2,s}:=\\operatorname{store}(b_2)$. If evaluation fails or storage is unavailable, return $(y_n,\\texttt{stage-2-input-or-representation-failure})$. Solve $W_sq_2=b_{2,s}$ with the same factor object and tolerance $\\varepsilon_2$. If this solve is not certified, return $(y_n,\\text{stage-2 status})$.\n4. Set $k_2=q_2+k_1$ and return $(y_n+h k_2,\\texttt{success},\\rho_1,\\rho_2)$ with the two certified update-residual bounds.\n\nThe exact-J branch takes $\\widetilde J_n=D_yf(t_n,y_n)$ and $\\widetilde T_n=\\partial_t f(t_n,y_n)$. The W-method branch permits any bounded matrix approximations satisfying the hypotheses of the local and global order results; it receives no scalar A/L-stability guarantee from the exact-J result. The factor matrix is the same in both stages, so the procedure performs one shifted-matrix factorization and two stage solves. For this mixed-arithmetic specification, field evaluations and outer affine combinations are exact real operations, and every matrix/RHS passed to QR must pass `store`; factorization, triangular solves, and residual checks use the operationwise model. A full hardware-rounding theorem for outer operations would need additional certified evaluation and update-error terms. A finite arithmetic residual is not a forward-error bound without an inverse estimate.\n",
+          "quote": "**Input:** an ODE field $f:D\\to\\mathbb R^d$ on the open domain $D\\subseteq\\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R^d$, $(t_n,y_n)\\in D$, $h>0$, a finite real state-Jacobian approximation $\\widetilde J_n$, a finite approximation $\\widetilde T_n$ to $\\partial_t f(t_n,y_n)$, stage residual tolerances $\\varepsilon_1,\\varepsilon_2\\ge0$, a unit roundoff $u>0$ with $du<1$, the selected stored finite set $\\mathbb F$ of [[def-operationwise-real-complex-floating-point-model-with-sqrt-and-fma]], a terminating field-evaluation routine `evaluate_f` that returns either the exact value $f(t,y)$ or `evaluation-failure` for each requested $(t,y)\\in D$, terminating `store` routine, and terminating certificate evaluator `evaluator` meeting the input contract of [[alg-rosenbrock-shifted-qr-solver-with-residual-certificate]]. The exact-storage check returns $v$ if every entry is in $\\mathbb F$ and `unavailable` otherwise; it does not round. Put\n$$\\alpha:=\\frac{1}{2+\\sqrt2}=1-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2},\\qquad W:=I_d-\\alpha h\\widetilde J_n.$$\nThe two-stage method is\n$$W k_1=f(t_n,y_n)+\\alpha h\\widetilde T_n,$$\n$$F_1=f\\!\\left(t_n+\\frac h2,y_n+\\frac h2k_1\\right),\\qquad W q_2=F_1-k_1,\\qquad k_2=q_2+k_1,$$\n$$y_{n+1}=y_n+h k_2.$$\n\nThe residuals $Wk_1-[f(t_n,y_n)+\\alpha h\\widetilde T_n]$ and $Wq_2-(F_1-k_1)$ are update residuals of their linear stage equations in the sense of [[def-implicit-residual-and-algebraic-error-units]]. The coefficient $\\alpha$ and the autonomous two-stage formula are the second-order Rosenbrock formula recorded for ode23s in WIAS Example 2.37. The first-stage $\\alpha h\\widetilde T_n$ term specifies the nonautonomous extension used here; its order is independently established under the stated boundedness hypotheses.\n\n1. Form $W$ by exact outer arithmetic and set $W_s:=\\operatorname{store}(W)$. If storage returns `unavailable`, return $(y_n,\\texttt{representation-unavailable})$. Call `factor(W_s,u,evaluator)` of [[alg-rosenbrock-shifted-qr-solver-with-residual-certificate]]. If it returns a factorization failure status, return $(y_n,\\texttt{factorization-failure})$; otherwise retain its factor object.\n2. Call `evaluate_f(t_n,y_n)`. If it returns `evaluation-failure`, return $(y_n,\\texttt{input-or-representation-failure})$. Otherwise denote its returned exact value by $f_0$, form $b_1=f_0+\\alpha h\\widetilde T_n$ by exact outer arithmetic, and set $b_{1,s}:=\\operatorname{store}(b_1)$. If storage returns `unavailable`, return $(y_n,\\texttt{input-or-representation-failure})$. Call the retained factor object's `solve(b_{1,s},\\varepsilon_1)`. If it is not certified, return $(y_n,\\text{stage-1 status})$. Otherwise set $k_1$ to its returned vector.\n3. Check $(t_n+h/2,y_n+hk_1/2)\\in D$. If not, return $(y_n,\\texttt{invalid-stage-domain})$. Otherwise call `evaluate_f(t_n+h/2,y_n+hk_1/2)`. If it returns `evaluation-failure`, return $(y_n,\\texttt{stage-2-input-or-representation-failure})$. Otherwise set $F_1$ to the returned exact value, form $b_2=F_1-k_1$ exactly, and set $b_{2,s}:=\\operatorname{store}(b_2)$. If storage returns `unavailable`, return $(y_n,\\texttt{stage-2-input-or-representation-failure})$. Call the same factor object's `solve(b_{2,s},\\varepsilon_2)`. If this solve is not certified, return $(y_n,\\text{stage-2 status})$.\n4. Set $k_2=q_2+k_1$ and return $(y_n+h k_2,\\texttt{success},\\rho_1,\\rho_2)$ with the two certified update-residual bounds.\n\nThe exact-J branch takes $\\widetilde J_n=D_yf(t_n,y_n)$ and $\\widetilde T_n=\\partial_t f(t_n,y_n)$. The W-method branch permits any bounded matrix approximations satisfying the hypotheses of the local and global order results; it receives no scalar A/L-stability guarantee from the exact-J result. The factor matrix is the same in both stages, so the procedure performs one shifted-matrix factorization and two stage solves. For this mixed-arithmetic specification, field evaluations and outer affine combinations are exact real operations, and every matrix/RHS passed to factorization or solve must pass `store`; factorization, triangular solves, and residual dot products use the operationwise model, while residual-bound formation is delegated to the terminating certificate evaluator. A full hardware-rounding theorem for outer operations would need additional certified evaluation and update-error terms. A finite arithmetic residual is not a forward-error bound without an inverse estimate.",
