-          "quote": "For the exact-Jacobian one-stage method with parameter $\\gamma>0$, apply the step to $y'=\\lambda y$ with $z=h\\lambda$. Its scalar stability function is\n$$R_\\gamma(z)=1+\\frac{z}{1-\\gamma z}=\\frac{1+(1-\\gamma)z}{1-\\gamma z}.$$\nIt has no pole in $\\operatorname{Re}z\\le0$ and satisfies $|R_\\gamma(z)|\\le1$ throughout that half-plane if and only if $\\gamma\\ge\\tfrac12$. Among these parameters, it is L-stable if and only if $\\gamma=1$. In particular, the named Rosenbrock–Euler case $\\gamma=1$ has the backward-Euler factor $R(z)=(1-z)^{-1}$.\n\n",
+          "quote": "For scalar stability, consider the exact-arithmetic complexification of the exact-Jacobian one-stage equation. For $\\lambda,y,k\\in\\mathbb C$, $h>0$, $\\gamma>0$, and $z=h\\lambda$, use $(1-\\gamma z)k=\\lambda y$ and $y_+=y+hk$. Equivalently, identify $\\mathbb C$ with $\\mathbb R^2$ and multiplication by $\\lambda$ with the real matrix $C_\\lambda=\\begin{pmatrix}\\operatorname{Re}\\lambda&-\\operatorname{Im}\\lambda\\\\\\operatorname{Im}\\lambda&\\operatorname{Re}\\lambda\\end{pmatrix}$; the corresponding real exact Jacobian is $C_\\lambda$, and the equations become $(I_2-\\gamma h C_\\lambda)k=C_\\lambda y$ and $y_+=y+hk$. This is a scalar stability analysis of exact stage equations; it is not an execution of the finite-precision real algorithm [[alg-rosenbrock-euler-step]]. Use the scalar rational-function criteria in [[def-scalar-rk-a-and-l-stability]] for its amplification function. Its scalar stability function is\n$$R_\\gamma(z)=1+\\frac{z}{1-\\gamma z}=\\frac{1+(1-\\gamma)z}{1-\\gamma z}.$$\nIt has no pole in $\\operatorname{Re}z\\le0$ and satisfies $|R_\\gamma(z)|\\le1$ throughout that half-plane if and only if $\\gamma\\ge\\tfrac12$. Among these parameters, it is L-stable if and only if $\\gamma=1$. In particular, the named Rosenbrock–Euler case $\\gamma=1$ has the backward-Euler factor $R(z)=(1-z)^{-1}$.",
